# <app> auto-deploy -- oneshot service that runs deploy-pull.sh once.
#
# Copy to `deploy/<app>-deploy.service` in your app repo, adapt <app>/<user>/paths, and install
# on the box as a SYSTEM unit under /etc/systemd/system/. Install steps are under "Installing the
# pull timer" in the template README -- relative paths won't resolve from your repo:
#   https://github.com/Rackbops/rackbops-web-deploy-template/blob/main/servers/nginx-static/README.md
#
# It runs as a system unit but drops to <user> via User= below -- that drop-to-user form is exactly
# why it's a system unit, not a `--user` one. Dropping to a DIFFERENT user is the part an ordinary
# user manager can't do: systemd.exec says that for "user services of any other user" than root,
# "switching user identity is not permitted, hence the only valid setting is the same user the
# user's service manager is running as". So User= is accepted in a user unit, it just can't name
# anyone else there. Paired with the .timer, which schedules it. The service body stays
# privilege-light: no docker, no sudo -- it only pulls and logs (installing the unit needs sudo;
# running it does not).
#
# No Environment=HOME= line: systemd.exec's SetLoginEnvironment= "defaults to true if User= ...
# [is] set", so $HOME is set automatically from User= above -- an explicit line here is redundant.
#
# TimeoutStartSec= matters because Type=oneshot disables systemd's start timeout by DEFAULT (unlike
# every other service type, per systemd.service(5)) -- without it, a hung `git pull` (a
# stalled-but-open network session with no keepalive to catch it -- SSH or HTTPS, the box clones
# over either) can park this unit running forever, and since the paired .timer's OnUnitActiveSec=
# only re-arms from the last *activation*, an already-running unit just... keeps running, with no
# visible error. On timeout systemd marks the unit failed and kills its cgroup, letting the timer's
# next cycle try again -- sufficient on its own, though a consumer can also add a faster-triggering
# complementary guard: ConnectTimeout=/ServerAliveInterval=/ServerAliveCountMax= to whatever SSH
# alias deploy-pull.sh's git pull uses over SSH, or http.lowSpeedLimit/http.lowSpeedTime for an
# HTTPS clone (see the template README's pull-model section for both).
#
# The 4min default has no basis beyond suiting a small hand-authored one-pager (the reference
# consumer) -- raise it for a bigger repo or a slow uplink. Git's smart-protocol fetch has no
# resume: a pull that legitimately needs longer than the cap gets killed, retried next tick, and
# killed again -- every attempt re-negotiates and re-downloads that fetch's own pack from zero (the
# delta since the last successful pull, not the whole repo history -- but still non-resumable), so
# it never completes. That is a livelock, not a slow deploy, and a single failed run looks identical
# in the journal to the one-off hang this setting exists to catch.
#
# Raising it does not need to stay under the paired .timer's OnUnitActiveSec= -- as noted above,
# that re-arms only from the unit's last activation, not per-tick, so a tick landing mid-run is
# simply skipped rather than queued alongside the one already running. Once that run ends (success,
# or this timeout's own kill), the schedule is already overdue and the timer's own coalescing
# (bounded by its AccuracySec=, 1min by default here since it's unset) catches it up -- a longer
# timeout only delays how soon a genuine hang gets caught, it doesn't add a second, separate stall.
# That's the documented systemd.timer(5) behavior for OnUnitActiveSec=; an open systemd issue
# (systemd/systemd#21600) flags some ambiguity specifically for a Type=oneshot unit with no
# RemainAfterExit= -- exactly this unit's shape -- so treat "catches up" as the expected behavior,
# not a guarantee verified against this exact unit on a real box.
#
# A timeout kill (or any interruption) mid-CHECKOUT can leave the worktree half-applied -- tracked
# files not matching HEAD, or an untracked file colliding with a path the incoming commit adds, or a
# stale .git/index.lock under SIGKILL -- and the web root is served straight off that worktree.
# Detecting that, the loud-vs-quiet shapes, and the `git reset --hard @{u}` recovery are all
# deploy-pull.sh's concern, not this unit's: see its pull-failure block and its post-pull drift NOTE
# (and the README's "#updating"). Don't restate them here -- the copies drift.

[Unit]
Description=<app> -- pull the latest site from GitHub
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
TimeoutStartSec=4min
User=<user>
ExecStart=/bin/bash /opt/stacks/<app>/deploy/deploy-pull.sh
